
 here are key ideas & strategies for handling exceptions in a system design or microservices round:

💡 1️⃣ Centralized Exception Handling

Use a Global Exception Handler (e.g., @ControllerAdvice in Spring Boot).

All exceptions — validation, business logic, or runtime — are caught centrally.

Return consistent error responses across all APIs (same JSON format).

💡 2️⃣ Custom Exception Hierarchy

Create different exception types:

ValidationException → Bad Request (400)

ResourceNotFoundException → Not Found (404)

BusinessRuleException → Conflict (409)

SystemException → Internal Server Error (500)

This improves readability and maps errors to correct HTTP codes.

💡 3️⃣ Meaningful Error Response Format

Always return structured errors:

{
  "timestamp": "2025-10-19T12:30:00",
  "errorCode": "USER_404",
  "message": "User not found",
  "details": "uri=/api/users/45"
}


✅ Easy for frontend and other microservices to parse.
✅ Helpful for observability.

💡 4️⃣ Logging and Monitoring

Log all exceptions with correlation IDs (for tracing across microservices).

Integrate with tools like ELK, Grafana, or Sentry for alerting.

Log stack traces only in internal systems, not in client responses.

💡 5️⃣ Retry and Circuit Breaker Patterns

For transient failures (e.g., DB timeout, service unavailability):

Use Retry (Spring Retry, Resilience4j Retry).

Use Circuit Breaker (Resilience4j / Hystrix) to prevent cascading failures.

For failed requests, send to a Dead Letter Queue (DLQ).

💡 6️⃣ Validation Errors

Use @Valid and @ExceptionHandler(MethodArgumentNotValidException.class)

Return clear validation messages — don’t just say “Bad Request”.

💡 7️⃣ Microservices Communication

When one service calls another:

Propagate errors with meaningful messages and HTTP codes.

Wrap external API calls with try-catch + fallback.

Standardize error formats across all services.

💡 8️⃣ Asynchronous / Background Jobs

Handle async exceptions via CompletableFuture.exceptionally() or error queues.

Store failed jobs for retry.

💡 9️⃣ Security & Validation

Never expose stack traces or internal messages to clients.

Log technical errors internally, return user-friendly messages externally.

💡 🔟 Documentation

Maintain a clear error code catalog:

Error Code	Meaning	HTTP Status
USER_404	User not found	404
ORDER_409	Duplicate order	409
SYS_500	Unexpected error	500

